<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Krittibas Ojha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 1400</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Krittivas Ojha, Krittivasa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Krittibas Ojha was a Bengali poet who wrote the Krittivasi Ramayan, a free retelling of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> in Bengali verse, probably in the early fifteenth century. Written in simple language and adding episodes from Bengali devotion, it became the most popular version of the epic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, recited and read in households for centuries, and helped establish Bengali as a literary language.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/krittibas-ojha.jpg" alt="Krittibas Ojha" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:Mukherjiavijit · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Krittibas_Memorial_ver1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
